Set-Content .gitignore @"
# Build outputs
build/
out/
bin/

# Gradle
.gradle/
!gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar

# IDE
.idea/
*.iml
.vscode/

# OS
.DS_Store

# Logs
*.log

# JMeter results
deploy/jmeter/results/


# Word temp files
~`$*
"@
*.docx
*.md
scaffold-services.ps1
!README.md

# The platform standards are tracked here - this repository is their source of truth, and each
# extracted service carries a copy. The .md ONLY: it is the source, it diffs and greps in review,
# and it is what a reviewer can actually read in a pull request. The .docx generated from it stays
# on disk and out of git, with every other generated deliverable - a .docx is a zip, so its text is
# compressed and nothing inside one can be audited by grep. Regenerate the .docx from the .md and
# never the reverse; that discipline is what stopped the previous .docx falling three versions
# behind, and it still applies now that only the source is version controlled.
!MASTER_CODE_STANDARDS.md


# Generated docs folder (docx deliverables)
/docs/

# The platform standards travel with every service - see MASTER_CODE_STANDARDS.md. Regenerate the
# .docx from the .md rather than editing it, or the two drift and the .docx becomes the stale one.
!MASTER_CODE_STANDARDS.md

# The class reference is a handover document like the standards beside it, and the blanket *.md rule
# above had been hiding it: every copy existed on disk, none was tracked, so an edit to it could
# never reach a reviewer. Un-ignored by name for the same reason MASTER_CODE_STANDARDS.md is.
!SERVICE_CLASS_REFERENCE.md

# CONFIDENTIAL - never commit, in any repository. The governing RFP (GeM bid GEM/2025/B/6668497)
# is a local-only document. This rule sits AFTER every negation above so it wins: docs/*.docx is
# un-ignored in a service repository, which would otherwise let a copy dropped into docs/ be
# committed by accident.
*RFP*
*rfp*.docx
